text:
It's ten o'clock on Sunday night ;

The clock hangs in the corner;

But never mind it, now;

For to-night you won't be born.

The wind goes softly past me;
 The grass is still asleep;
 My spirit knows no rest,
 For it is bound to keep
 Its vow for evermore
 To love thee more than life!

"The years were rolling out like thunder-storms;
She had not known a single day of peace;
Her heart was torn asunder, she was dumb--
Only one thing could comfort or release."

The man who’ll marry her shall own the land;
the woman will own him for life, or death—
or death as well; he can have no wife else.

I came where many a soul had trod;
 I saw old friends on ancient ground,
 The dead still loved them, though they were cold.

 I heard what others spoke long since;
 Yet felt like one who never was,
 Whose heart shall know no joy or pain.
